you know my latest obsession  is this concept of finite and infinite  games and i've i've mentioned finite  mindedness or infinite mindedness a  couple times while we've been discussing  this goes uh back to the mid-1980s uh a  philosopher and theologian by the name  of dr james karst defined these two  types of games finite games and infinite  games a finite game is defined as known  players  fixed rules and an agreed upon objective  football there's always a beginning  middle and an end  and if there's a winner that means there  have to be losers  then there are infinite games infinite  games are defined as known and unknown  players which means new players can join  at any time  and sometimes you don't even know who  all the other players are  the  the rules are changeable which means  every player can play however they want  and the objective is to perpetuate the  game to stay in the game as long as  possible  we are players in infinite games every  day of our lives whether we know it or  not nobody wins education for example  you know you can come in first for the  finite amount of time you're at school  when we agree upon the time frame the  school year and we agreed upon the  metric grades  but nobody wins education  uh um and likewise nobody wins business  there's no such thing as winning  business it doesn't exist right but when  we listen to the language of so many of  our leaders it becomes abundantly clear  that they don't always know the game  they're playing in they talk about being  number one  being the best or beating their  competition  based on what  based on what agreed upon time frames  metrics and objectives and this is a  problem because when we play with a  finite mindset in an infinite game when  we play to win in a game that has no  finish line there are actually some very  predictable and consistent outcomes the  big one being the decline of trust the  decline of cooperation and the decline  of innovation and so we actually have to  adjust our mindset to fit the game that  we're playing and in the case of cyber  security you know you're you're actually  trying to prevent crime from happening  the criminals aren't trying to beat you  they're trying to do more they're just  trying to get more money  they're just trying to trick people they  just want access they actually don't  care about you and your cybersecurity  they're just trying to do more crime  right it's an infinite game  and you don't even know who all the  criminals are there's no such thing as  winning cyber security it doesn't exist  it's an ongoing cat and mouse game and  so you have to have the right mindset  for the game you're playing and in this  case it's not about winning it's about  constant improvement and it goes back to  that analogy that we discussed right at  the beginning when we talked about the  lifestyle of business versus trying to  treat it like a finite game right which  is it's not the end of the the financial  year doesn't symbolize the end of the  game  it's just a marker it's just a waypoint  and so all of these things that you and  i have been discussing is all about  constant improvement you'll never be  perfect at any of it ever that's not the  point the point is that you're  constantly working to advance and you're  constantly saying how can we be better  how can we better relative not only to  our to the other players in the game but  also relative to the criminals in the  game because  you could be the best quote unquote uh  compared to all of all of your uh of the  other companies in the marketplace but  if the criminals are smarter than you  then then there's no point  um so so i think that's that's to adjust  our mindset to play for the game we're  in which is the game of business and  especially cyber security which really  is an infinite game  i think is really really important
 

